WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.962
OT security monitoring is the continuing observation and analysis of industrial systems,

00:00:04.962 --> 00:00:09.014
networks, identities, and operational activity for evidence of security-relevant change.

00:00:09.014 --> 00:00:13.471
It helps defenders understand what is communicating, what actions are occurring,

00:00:13.471 --> 00:00:16.712
and whether current behavior matches the expected operation.

00:00:16.712 --> 00:00:20.764
Visibility may include network conversations among controllers, HMIs, engineering workstations,

00:00:20.764 --> 00:00:22.384
historians, and remote-access systems.

00:00:22.384 --> 00:00:26.841
It can also include authentication, firewall, endpoint, application, and configuration events.

00:00:26.841 --> 00:00:29.678
No single source provides the complete picture,

00:00:29.678 --> 00:00:32.514
so monitoring combines evidence from several layers.

00:00:32.514 --> 00:00:37.781
OT monitoring differs from ordinary enterprise monitoring because technical activity has process meaning.

00:00:37.781 --> 00:00:43.048
A command may be expected during a maintenance window and concerning during production.

00:00:43.048 --> 00:00:47.910
A communication loss may represent a cyber event, equipment failure, network fault,

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:49.126
or planned shutdown.

00:00:49.126 --> 00:00:53.988
Operators and engineers provide context that security telemetry cannot create by itself.

00:00:53.988 --> 00:00:57.634
Safety, reliability, and availability constrain how monitoring is deployed.

00:00:57.634 --> 00:01:01.281
Active probes and untested agents may disrupt sensitive devices.

00:01:01.281 --> 00:01:04.928
Passive collection and carefully placed sensors are often preferred,

00:01:04.928 --> 00:01:06.548
especially near control networks.

00:01:06.548 --> 00:01:11.816
Monitoring should never create an uncontrolled path or a single point of failure.

00:01:11.816 --> 00:01:17.488
The purpose is not to collect every packet forever or generate the most alerts.

00:01:17.488 --> 00:01:21.540
Effective monitoring establishes asset and behavior visibility, detects meaningful deviation,

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:24.376
supports investigation, preserves evidence, and informs response.

00:01:24.376 --> 00:01:29.238
It becomes valuable when the organization can explain what an observation means,

00:01:29.238 --> 00:01:34.100
who reviews it, and what action follows without jeopardizing the physical process.
